//! lotus/soft_drive.rye — ALES79: the soft-clip overdrive — ALES78's hard clip with its corner rounded. The hard-clip drive (ALES78) pins
//! a boosted sample abruptly at ±ceil: the transition from clean to clipped is a sharp corner, and a sharp corner is bright and buzzy — the
//! sound of a fuzz. An overdrive is gentler: below a KNEE the boosted signal passes clean, and between the knee and the ceiling the slope
//! HALVES, so the wave bends toward the ceiling rather than slamming into it. That rounded shoulder is the warmth of an overdriven tube or a
//! pushed preamp — a softer harmonic spectrum than the hard clip's, the same odd-harmonic character with the highest edges smoothed.
//!
//! The crux is that SOFT-CLIP DRIVE IS ALES78's HARD CLIP WITH ITS CORNER ROUNDED — and a hard clip is the soft clip whose KNEE sits AT the
//! ceiling. The two rungs are one waveshaper read at two knee positions: pull the knee below the ceiling and a half-slope shoulder opens between
//! them; push the knee up to the ceiling and the shoulder vanishes, leaving ALES78's sharp pin. So the pre-gain is ALES78's verbatim (ALES6's
//! num/den into i64), and the shape is one piecewise-linear map on the boosted magnitude m = |boosted|, sign held: m ≤ knee passes clean; knee <
//! m rises at half slope, knee + (m − knee)/2; and once that shoulder would reach the ceiling (m ≥ 2·ceil − knee) it pins to ceil, ALES78's own
//! ceiling law at the shoulder's top. Three straight segments — slope one, slope one-half, slope zero — every one exact in integer arithmetic.
//!
//! The output magnitude never exceeds the ceiling (the shoulder is capped, the pin holds), so the write lands in [−ceil, ceil] ⊆ the i16 rail
//! BEFORE the write, exactly as the hard clip: the saturate that follows is a documented no-op, the shape does the clipping. The map is ODD,
//! soft_drive(−x) = −soft_drive(x) (the magnitude is shaped, the sign carried through), so a symmetric input yields a symmetric output — odd
//! harmonics, softened. It is MEMORYLESS: every output depends only on its own input, so the in-place read-then-write touches nothing another
//! step needs.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES79.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-205812_fill-ales79-lotus-soft-clip-overdrive.md
//! Stands on: lotus/drive.rye (ALES78) — the pre-gain and the ceiling pin, reused with a knee opened below the ceiling; lotus/fader.rye (ALES6)
//! — the num/den gain in i64; lotus/limit.rye (ALES49) — the ceiling law the shoulder's top reuses; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip,
//! sample_min/max, the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no
//! keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the gain is a rational num/den (unity = 1/1), the knee and ceiling magnitudes in sample
//! units (not decibels), and the shape a piecewise-linear waveshaper, instantaneous (no attack/release, no anti-aliasing — the harmonics fold in
//! the i16 domain exactly as any integer waveshaper's). One multiply, one divide, and a bounded piecewise map per sample; the gain runs in i64
//! so it never overflows before the shape, and the shaped value fits the i16 rail by construction.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/soft_drive.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const drive = @import("drive.rye");

pub const soft_drive_version = "20260814.205812";

/// The soft-clip drive's faults, one consistent name each. BadGain, BadCeiling, and BadRange coincide by name with ALES78's hard drive (and
/// through it ALES49's limiter) — Zig merges error sets by name — so a composer sees one consistent fault whichever drive refuses. BadKnee is
/// the one name this rung adds: the knee must be a legal magnitude at or below the ceiling, so the soft shoulder sits inside the field (a knee
/// AT the ceiling is legal and degenerates to the hard clip).
pub const SoftDriveError = error{
    /// A zero denominator, a gain below unity (num < den — a drive boosts, never attenuates), or a numerator past drive.max_drive_num — no write.
    BadGain,
    /// A knee below one sample unit, or above the ceiling (the shoulder would sit outside the field) — refused, no write.
    BadKnee,
    /// A ceiling of zero, or above sample_max (nothing boosted could ever be pinned there) — refused, no write.
    BadCeiling,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// The soft-clip shape on one non-negative boosted magnitude `m`: below `knee` it passes clean; between `knee` and the shoulder's top it rises at
/// half slope, `knee + (m − knee)/2`; at or past the top (`m ≥ 2·ceil − knee`) it pins to `ceil`. Returns a magnitude in [0, ceil]. Total and
/// small so the per-sample loop reads plainly; the sign is carried by the caller.
fn shape_magnitude(m: i64, knee: i64, ceil: i64) i64 {
    // invariant: the inputs are legal magnitudes with the knee inside the field — 0 ≤ m, 1 ≤ knee ≤ ceil ≤ sample_max — so every segment below
    // is exact and non-negative.
    assert(m >= 0 and knee >= 1 and knee <= ceil and ceil <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
    if (m <= knee) return m;
    // The half-slope shoulder: the excess above the knee counts half. @divTrunc toward zero, exact for a non-negative excess.
    const shaped: i64 = knee + @divTrunc(m - knee, 2);
    // invariant: the shoulder never rises above the ceiling before the pin — it reaches ceil exactly at m = 2·ceil − knee, and this branch is
    // reached only for m > knee, so shaped ≥ knee.
    assert(shaped >= knee);
    if (shaped > ceil) return ceil;
    // invariant: a shaped shoulder value sits in [knee, ceil] — clean below, capped above.
    assert(shaped >= knee and shaped <= ceil);
    return shaped;
}

/// Soft-drive `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: each sample is boosted by the rational gain `num`/`den` (at least unity)
/// and then soft-clipped through a knee — clean below `knee`, half-slope between `knee` and `ceil`, pinned at `ceil` above. A hard clip is this
/// same map with `knee == ceil`. After the call every written sample's magnitude is at most `ceil`, its sign matches the input's, and every
/// output depends only on its own input (the shape is memoryless). Refuses BadGain, BadKnee (a knee outside [1, ceil]), BadCeiling, and BadRange
/// — each BEFORE any write, so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only — never the length.
pub fn soft_drive(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, num: u32, den: u32, knee: i32, ceil: i32) SoftDriveError!void {
    // invariant: the gain is legal exactly as ALES78's hard drive — nonzero denominator, at least unity, numerator within the safe bound. The
    // same three checks, so the same three faults, before any write.
    if (den == 0) return error.BadGain;
    if (num < den) return error.BadGain;
    if (num > drive.max_drive_num) return error.BadGain;
    // invariant: the ceiling is a legal magnitude — at least one sample unit, no greater than the i16 rail (ALES49's exact bound).
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: the knee is a legal magnitude sitting inside the field — at least one sample unit and no greater than the ceiling, so the soft
    // shoulder [knee, ceil] is well-formed (a knee AT the ceiling is legal and degenerates to the hard clip).
    if (knee < 1) return error.BadKnee;
    if (knee > ceil) return error.BadKnee;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const ceiling: i64 = @as(i64, ceil); // safe: 1 <= ceil <= sample_max
    const knee_v: i64 = @as(i64, knee); // safe: 1 <= knee <= ceil
    const n: i64 = @as(i64, num);
    const d: i64 = @as(i64, den);
    // invariant: the gain terms are bounded and positive, and the knee sits inside the field — so the pre-gain never overflows i64 and the
    // shape's segments are all exact.
    assert(n >= d and d >= 1 and n <= @as(i64, drive.max_drive_num) and knee_v >= 1 and knee_v <= ceiling);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const dry: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]);
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and dry <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // The pre-gain, ALES78's verbatim: dry boosted by num/den in i64, @divTrunc toward zero exactly as ALES6's fader.
        const boosted: i64 = @divTrunc(dry * n, d);
        // Shape the MAGNITUDE and carry the sign — the waveshaper is odd, so a symmetric input maps to a symmetric output.
        const mag: i64 = if (boosted < 0) -boosted else boosted;
        const shaped_mag: i64 = shape_magnitude(mag, knee_v, ceiling);
        const shaped: i64 = if (boosted < 0) -shaped_mag else shaped_mag;
        // invariant: the shaped value lands within [−ceil, ceil] ⊆ [sample_min, sample_max], so the write fits the i16 rail BY CONSTRUCTION —
        // the shape does the clipping, and the saturate that follows is a documented no-op over an already-bounded value.
        assert(shaped >= -ceiling and shaped <= ceiling);
        // invariant: the sign is held — the magnitude is shaped and the boosted sign carried, so an odd waveshaper never flips a sample.
        assert((boosted >= 0 and shaped >= 0) or (boosted <= 0 and shaped <= 0));
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(shaped);
    }
    // invariant: a soft drive changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: A KNEE AT THE CEILING IS ALES78's HARD CLIP (the family recognition, proven byte-for-byte against the real hard drive); the
//     HALF-SLOPE SHOULDER matches a hand computation exactly (clean below the knee, half slope to the top, pinned above); a WIDE-KNEE overdrive
//     is GENTLER than the hard clip at the same gain (a shoulder value sits below the hard pin wherever they differ); the shape is ODD,
//     soft_drive(−x) = −soft_drive(x); the OUTPUT NEVER EXCEEDS THE CEILING under a heavy drive; the span discipline holds; and each fault —
//     BadGain, BadKnee (below 1, above ceil), BadCeiling, BadRange — refuses by name, the clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. A KNEE AT THE CEILING IS ALES78's HARD CLIP — the crux. With knee == ceil the shoulder vanishes and the soft drive reproduces the hard
    //    drive byte-for-byte, at the same gain and ceiling, over a signal that both passes and clips. Proven against the REAL ALES78 (both run).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 1000, -2000, 5000, -6000, 100, -50 };
        var a = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&a, &sig);
        var b = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&b, &sig);
        try soft_drive(&a, 0, a.len, 4, 1, 1500, 1500); // knee AT the ceiling
        try drive.drive(&b, 0, b.len, 4, 1, 1500); // ALES78's hard clip
        if (!eq(a.samples(), b.samples())) return error.KneeAtCeilNotHard;
    }

    // 2. THE HALF-SLOPE SHOULDER — a hand computation. knee = 1000, ceil = 1500 (shoulder top at 2·1500 − 1000 = 2000), gain ×1/1 so the
    //    boosted value is the input. 800 passes (clean); 1000 passes (at the knee); 1400 → 1000 + 400/2 = 1200; 1800 → 1000 + 800/2 = 1400;
    //    2000 → 1000 + 1000/2 = 1500 (reaches the ceiling exactly); 3000 → pinned to 1500. Signs carried on the negatives.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 800, -1000, 1400, -1800, 2000, -3000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1, 1, 1000, 1500);
        const want = [_]i16{ 800, -1000, 1200, -1400, 1500, -1500 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.ShoulderWrong;
    }

    // 3. A WIDE-KNEE OVERDRIVE IS GENTLER THAN THE HARD CLIP — at the same gain and ceiling, wherever the two differ the soft shoulder sits
    //    strictly below the hard pin (a rounded corner, never louder than the abrupt one). Proven sample-by-sample against the real hard drive.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 200, -400, 900, -1300, 2500, -4000, 1100, -1150 };
        var soft = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&soft, &sig);
        var hard = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&hard, &sig);
        try soft_drive(&soft, 0, soft.len, 2, 1, 800, 1500); // knee 800 well below ceil 1500
        try drive.drive(&hard, 0, hard.len, 2, 1, 1500);
        const so = soft.samples();
        const ho = hard.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < so.len) : (i += 1) {
            const sm: i16 = if (so[i] < 0) -so[i] else so[i];
            const hm: i16 = if (ho[i] < 0) -ho[i] else ho[i];
            if (sm > hm) return error.SoftLouderThanHard; // the shoulder is never louder than the pin
        }
    }

    // 4. THE SHAPE IS ODD — soft_drive(−x) = −soft_drive(x). A ± symmetric input maps to a ± symmetric output at a wide knee: out[0] = −out[1],
    //    out[2] = −out[3], out[4] = −out[5] — the shoulder shapes the magnitude and carries the sign, so only odd harmonics arise.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 700, -700, 3000, -3000, 60, -60 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 3, 1, 900, 1400);
        const out = clip.samples();
        if (out[0] != -out[1] or out[2] != -out[3] or out[4] != -out[5]) return error.NotOddSymmetric;
    }

    // 5. THE OUTPUT NEVER EXCEEDS THE CEILING — under a heavy ×8 drive every output magnitude stays at or below the ceiling, its sign held. The
    //    shoulder caps and the pin holds, so no shaped value can cross the rail.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -100, 4000, -4000, 20000, -20000, 5, -5 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const ceil: i16 = 1200;
        try soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 8, 1, 600, ceil);
        const out = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (out[i] > ceil or out[i] < -ceil) return error.CeilingCrossed;
            const s = sig[i];
            if (!((s >= 0 and out[i] >= 0) or (s <= 0 and out[i] <= 0))) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 6. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — soft-driving a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the
    //    whole suite keeps. Drive the middle two of six; the first and last two are untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 5000, -5000, 300, 400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try soft_drive(&clip, 2, 2, 4, 1, 800, 1000); // indices 2,3 only
        const want = [_]i16{ 100, 200, 1000, -1000, 300, 400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 7a. A ZERO DENOMINATOR / BELOW-UNITY GAIN / OVERSIZED NUMERATOR each refuse BadGain — before any write, the clip untouched (ALES78's gain
    //     faults, verbatim).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 0, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroDenTouched;
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1, 2, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.BelowUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongBelowUnity;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedBelowUnityTouched;
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, drive.max_drive_num + 1, 1, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.WideNumSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongWideNum;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedWideNumTouched;
    }

    // 7b. A KNEE below one sample unit refuses BadKnee; a knee ABOVE the ceiling refuses BadKnee — before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 0, 1000)) |_| return error.ZeroKneeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadKnee) return error.WrongZeroKnee;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroKneeTouched;
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 1001, 1000)) |_| return error.HighKneeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadKnee) return error.WrongHighKnee;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedHighKneeTouched;
    }

    // 7c. A ZERO / OVER-RAIL CEILING refuses BadCeiling; a SPAN past the end refuses BadRange — before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, 10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 1, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroCeilSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroCeil;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedZeroCeilTouched;
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2, 1, 1, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1)) |_| return error.OverCeilSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverCeil;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverCeilTouched;
        if (soft_drive(&clip, 1, 3, 2, 1, 500, 1000)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-soft-drive: the SOFT-CLIP OVERDRIVE — ALES78's hard clip with its corner rounded. SOFT-CLIP DRIVE IS ALES78's HARD CLIP " ++
        "WITH ITS CORNER ROUNDED, and a hard clip is the soft clip whose KNEE sits AT the ceiling: below the knee the boosted signal passes " ++
        "clean, between knee and ceiling the slope HALVES (knee + (m − knee)/2), and once that shoulder reaches the ceiling (m ≥ 2·ceil − knee) " ++
        "it pins — three straight segments, slope one, one-half, zero, each exact in integers. The pre-gain is ALES78's verbatim (ALES6's " ++
        "num/den into i64), the ceiling law ALES49's at the shoulder's top. A knee AT the ceiling reproduces ALES78's hard drive byte-for-byte " ++
        "(proven against the real hard clip); a hand-computed half-slope shoulder matches exactly (800,1000 clean; 1400→1200; 1800→1400; " ++
        "2000→1500; 3000 pinned); a wide-knee overdrive is GENTLER than the hard clip at the same gain (the shoulder never louder than the " ++
        "pin); the shape is ODD, soft_drive(−x) = −soft_drive(x), so a symmetric input yields odd harmonics softened; the output never exceeds " ++
        "the ceiling under a heavy ×8 drive, every sign held; the span discipline holds; a zero den / below-unity gain / oversized numerator " ++
        "refuses BadGain, a knee below 1 or above the ceiling BadKnee, a zero or over-rail ceiling BadCeiling, a span past the end BadRange, " ++
        "each leaving the clip untouched. The shape lands every output in [−ceil, ceil] ⊆ the i16 rail BEFORE the write, so the shape does the " ++
        "clipping and the saturate is a documented no-op; one multiply, one divide, one bounded piecewise map per sample; no delay line, no " ++
        "anti-aliasing, no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. The DRIVE family now holds two corners, " ++
        "the sharp and the rounded — a wavefolder and an asymmetric tube are the next\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
